   "passage": "**The Ocean's Hidden Bill**\n\n(1) Since the Industrial Revolution, the ocean has absorbed roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide released by human activity. This uptake has slowed the warming of the atmosphere, but it has come at a chemical price. When carbon dioxide dissolves in seawater, it forms carbonic acid, which releases hydrogen ions. As a result, the average pH of surface seawater has fallen from about 8.2 to 8.1. The change looks small, yet because the pH scale is logarithmic, it represents an increase in acidity of roughly 30 percent.\n\n(2) The extra hydrogen ions do more than lower pH. They bind with carbonate ions, the very building blocks that corals, oysters, and tiny sea snails called pteropods use to construct their shells and skeletons. With fewer carbonate ions available, these organisms must spend more energy to build and maintain calcium carbonate structures. In laboratory experiments, pteropod shells placed in water with the chemistry projected for the year 2100 began to dissolve within about six weeks.\n\n(3) The consequences are not __confined__ to laboratories. In the late 2000s, oyster hatcheries on the Pacific coast of the United States lost billions of larvae. Researchers traced the losses to the upwelling of deep, carbon-rich water that was corrosive to young shells. The hatcheries survived by installing sensors and adding sodium carbonate to buffer incoming seawater during dangerous periods — a costly fix that works in a tank but not in the open sea.\n\n(4) Some scientists hope that coastal ecosystems can offer partial relief. Seagrass meadows and kelp forests absorb carbon dioxide during photosynthesis, which can raise the pH of the surrounding water during daylight hours. However, this effect is local and rises and falls between day and night. Ultimately, most researchers agree that the only lasting solution is to reduce the amount of carbon dioxide entering the ocean in the first place.",
