   "passage": "**Out of Place**\n\n(1) My grandfather repaired watches in a shop no wider than a doorway. For most of my childhood I thought it was the dullest place on earth: a single bulb, a wooden stool, and the endless ticking of other people's time.\n\n(2) The summer I failed my university entrance exam, my mother sent me there \"to be useful.\" I sat behind the counter and sulked while he bent over a pocket watch whose owner had died years before. The family, he said, only wanted it to run again.\n\n(3) \"Why bother?\" I asked. \"Nobody even needs it to tell the time.\"\n\n(4) He did not look up. He lifted a gear smaller than a grain of rice with his tweezers, held it to the light, and set it down again. \"A watch stops for one of two reasons,\" he said at last. \"Either something is broken, or something is out of place. Most people assume it is broken. Usually, it is only out of place.\"\n\n(5) I rolled my eyes, but I watched. For two afternoons he took the watch apart and laid every piece on a white cloth in neat rows, like students waiting for roll call. On the third afternoon he put it back together. Nothing new had been added. When he wound it, it began to tick, faint and steady, as if it had only been waiting for someone patient enough.\n\n(6) I said nothing then. But that evening I took out my exam papers, the ones I had pushed under my bed, and spread them across the floor in neat rows.",
