   "passage": "**The Option Nobody Wants**\n\n(1) Classical economics assumes that consumers judge each option on its own merits and choose the one that gives them the greatest value. Behavioral economists, however, have shown that people often judge an option by comparing it with whatever happens to sit beside it. One of the clearest demonstrations of this is the \"decoy effect.\"\n\n(2) In a well-known experiment described by economist Dan Ariely, 100 students were shown three subscription offers for a magazine: online-only access for $59, print-only for $125, and print plus online for $125. Because the print-only offer cost the same as the combined package but provided less, no one chose it. Yet its presence mattered, as the table below shows.\n\n| Offer | Price | Choices with 3 offers | Choices with 2 offers |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Online only | $59 | 16 students | 68 students |\n| Print only | $125 | 0 students | (not offered) |\n| Print + online | $125 | 84 students | 32 students |\n\n(3) When the print-only option was removed, the choices shifted dramatically. Nothing about the other two offers had changed — only the comparison had. The unwanted option worked as a decoy, making the combined package look like a bargain.\n\n(4) Businesses use decoys widely, from cinema popcorn sizes to phone storage plans. Critics argue that such pricing exploits predictable weaknesses in human judgment, while defenders reply that consumers remain free to choose. Either way, the research suggests that a \"rational\" choice may depend less on the options themselves than on how they are arranged.",
